 // the merges are timed for the 30 s line, with the longest merge and the oldest export pending
 // at a merge; "max N" in memexport-batching.txt merges once N exports are pending (1: each at
 // once, as without batching; 0: no limit).
+//
+// Fix 136c: Modern Warfare 2 with batching on drew multicoloured noise over concrete pillars,
+// which stayed after batching was switched off and after loading the checkpoint again (gone
+// after a restart). A merge writes guest memory, which invalidates the GPU's copy of those
+// pages. Merged late, it came after a later GPU write over the same pages (a resolve, an export
+// that is not read back): its invalidation threw that newer GPU data away and the pages were
+// uploaded again from guest memory, which lacks it, and stayed so (static data, never written
+// again). Read back at once, the merge always came first. Now any GPU write over a page of a
+// pending export, other than a batched export's own, merges the batch first (Overlaps), so the
+// order is the old one; the batched exports among themselves merge in their order as before.
